cctext patch 0006: synthetic bold and italic.

The bundled serif and mono families have no bold or italic face, so
font-weight: bold and font-style: italic drew them upright and regular.
When the face cache has no face with the style asked for, it now returns
a synthetic variant of the closest face (created once, owned by it,
sharing its font data): italic slants the outline by tan(12 deg)
(FreeType's FT_GlyphSlot_Oblique), bold overstrikes it with a second copy
size/24 .. size/32 to the right (Skia's fake-bold ratios; the copies are
filled non-zero, so they unite), advances unchanged. A face's own style
comes from its OS/2 weight / fsSelection and head.macStyle, and from its
registration. A fallback glyph (patch 0005) drawn for a bold or italic
face gets the synthetic style its own face lacks. Stroked text is slanted
but not overstruck. The text extents include the slant and overstrike.

Apply after 0005.

